“My Facebook page is enough” is one of the reasons small Moroccan businesses give most often for not having a website. It isn't absurd: many customers really do come from social networks. But it forgets what a page can't do.

## What social networks do well

- Show your activity day to day, in photos and videos.
- Keep in touch with your existing customers and the people around them.
- Receive messages quickly, often through WhatsApp.
- Reach a targeted audience through advertising.

## What a Facebook page doesn't do

- Get you found on Google. When someone types “plumber casablanca” or “dental clinic rabat”, websites and Google profiles come up, rarely a Facebook page.
- Guarantee your audience. The algorithm decides how many of your followers see each post, and that share falls without you being able to do anything.
- Protect you. A hacked or suspended account and everything disappears overnight: followers, messages and history.
- Present a complete offer: pages per service, prices, a structured catalog, online payment.
- Reassure a demanding customer. In B2B, health or consulting, the lack of a website shows the moment someone checks who you are.

## What a website adds

A website is the only place online that belongs to you. It's found on Google by people searching for your trade, it presents each service on its own page, it gathers your proof, and it turns a visit into a call or a WhatsApp message. And unlike a page, you know exactly how many people visited it and what they did there.

## The right pair: a website as the base, social networks as the amplifier

The businesses that do best use each tool for what it does well. Social networks attract attention and keep the relationship going; the website captures searches, reassures and converts. Every post points to a specific page on the website, and the website points back to your social networks and WhatsApp.

## And the budget?

A professional business website starts at MAD 1,899 at Wevtex, delivered in 7 to 14 days, in your name and on your domain. It's a one-off investment, where a social page needs time every week and never really belongs to you.

## Where to start

1. Create or take over your Google Business Profile: it's free and it's what comes up first on local searches.
2. Launch a simple website with a page per service, your contact details and a WhatsApp button.
3. Connect it to your social networks, and point your posts to the right pages.
